sections.s2_technical:
The key to this scenario is distinguishing two things that are usually confused: the *error rate metric* and *reliability*. The error rate is the fraction of client requests that return an error. Reliability is the system's sustained ability to keep producing correct outcomes for the requests it accepts. Retries affect these two in opposite directions when the bottleneck is a shared, saturated dependency.

When the payment dependency starts to saturate, the first failures appear. Retries kick in and resend those failed requests. From the client's point of view, many of those resends eventually succeed, so the visible error rate stays low. But under the surface, each original request is now generating multiple downstream calls. The dependency, already at its limit, is handed even more work — the exact work that is causing it to fail. This is a positive feedback loop: more failures cause more retries cause more load cause more failures. The error rate stays flat for a while and then falls off a cliff when the dependency collapses entirely.

```java
// Retries mask overload: visible errors stay low while downstream load triples
for (int attempt = 1; attempt <= 3; attempt++) {
    try { return paymentDep.charge(req); }  // succeeds "eventually" → low error rate...
    catch (Overloaded e) { /* resend → +1 load on the thing that is already drowning */ }
}
```

Now remove the retries. A request that hits the saturated dependency fails once and returns an error to the client immediately. The visible error rate jumps, because failures are no longer being papered over by eventual success. But three things improve. First, the downstream load drops back to one call per request, so the dependency is no longer amplified into collapse. Second, failing a fraction of requests early is a form of load shedding: the capacity those requests would have consumed is preserved for the majority, which continue to succeed correctly. Third, because the dependency is no longer being hammered, it recovers faster, so the elevated error rate is also shorter-lived.

```java
// No retry: each request is one downstream call. Some fail fast (error rate up),
// but the dependency is not amplified, so it recovers and the majority still succeed.
return paymentDep.charge(req); // one attempt; failure is surfaced honestly
```

The senior-level nuance is that this is not an argument against all retries — it is an argument against retries as a reflex and against measuring health by a single surface metric. Retries are safe and valuable when the failure is a genuine isolated blip and the dependency has spare capacity. They are actively harmful when the dependency is saturated, because then the retry is spending the very resource that is scarce. The principal engineer's move works because it replaces a metric-optimising behaviour (keep the error number low by any means) with a capacity-preserving behaviour (fail some requests honestly and early so the system as a whole stays up). The right long-term design is not "no retries" but "retries governed by a budget and a circuit breaker, plus explicit load shedding, plus health metrics that measure business outcomes rather than just the surface error rate."

A further nuance is observability. The reason the problem was invisible is that the dashboard measured the surface error rate, which the retries kept artificially healthy. The fix is to also measure the amplification factor — downstream calls per client request — and the dependency's saturation. Once you can see that one request is generating four downstream calls, the "healthy" error rate stops being reassuring and starts being a warning.

